{"status":"ok","message-type":"work-list","message-version":"1.0.0","message":{"facets":{},"total-results":3660182,"items":[{"DOI":"10.2139/ssrn.3648532","title":["Prudential Supervisory Disclosure (PSD) with Supervisory Technology (SupTech): Lessons from a FinTech Crisis"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2020]]},"type":"posted-content","abstract":"<jats:p>The U.S. financial markets faced an unprecedented rapid decline and recovery on May 6, 2010, known as the May 6 flash crash. Roughly one trillion $ market value in less than thirty minutes vanished with the biggest one-day point decline in the history of the DJIA at the time.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;Since the market events took place in electronic markets, and algorithmic trading (AT) and high-frequency trading (HFT), parts of FinTech, played significant roles, we handle the May 6 flash crash from the FinTech, SupTech, and financial supervision perspectives.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;With the flashback method, we analyzed the reactions of market participants, media, and two financial supervisors, the SEC, and the CFTC, to the market crash. We find that the technological imbalance between financial markets or institutions and their supervisors drove the markets in uncertainty, hence in a fear and panic environment. Since the imbalance has not diminished yet, the same risks still exist. As a remedy, we introduce a new concept and model with a well-functioning SupTech system to cope with the May 6 type FinTech crises.</jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.2139/ssrn.3632053","title":["Digitalisation of Financial Supervision with Supervisory Technology (SupTech)"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2020]]},"type":"posted-content","abstract":"<jats:p>In this article, we discuss and analyse the main components of a digital financial supervisory system with supervisory technology (SupTech). This work offers a new SupTech definition and configures the digital pillars of a financial supervisory system. We contribute to the TECHs in Finance (FinTech, RegTech, SupTech) literature with two new concepts: “prudential supervisory disclosure” and “sustainable finance technology”, or SuFTech. &lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;This work also touches on real TECHs in Finance cases from several countries. We find that the May 6, 2010 market crash at the U.S. financial markets, one of the biggest FinTech crises, addresses the importance of having a well-functioning SupTech system. The case also points out that even a leading technology-producing country or a developed country faces unprecedented FinTech crashes or crises unless the country’s financial supervisors keep pace with technology and develop a well-functioning SupTech system. &lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;</jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.1201/9788743811244-23","title":["Supervisory Technologies (SupTech)"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,17]]},"container-title":["The Fintech Matrix"],"type":"book-chapter"},{"DOI":"10.1057/s41310-021-00111-7","title":["Prudential supervisory disclosure (PSD) with supervisory technology (SupTech): lessons from a FinTech crisis"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2021,2,24]]},"container-title":["International Journal of Disclosure and Governance"],"type":"journal-article","abstract":"<jats:title>Abstract</jats:title>
                  <jats:p>The U.S. financial markets faced an unprecedented rapid decline and recovery on May 6, 2010, known as the May 6 flash crash. Roughly one trillion $ market value in less than thirty minutes vanished with the biggest one-day point decline in the history of the DJIA at the time. Since the market events took place in electronic markets, and algorithmic trading and high-frequency trading, parts of FinTech, played significant roles, we handle the May 6 flash crash from the FinTech, SupTech, and financial supervision perspectives. With the flashback method, we analyzed the reactions of market participants, media, and two financial supervisors, the SEC, and the CFTC, to the market crash. We find that the technological imbalance between financial markets or institutions and their supervisors drove the markets in uncertainty, hence in a fear and panic environment. Since the imbalance has not diminished yet, the same risks still exist. As a remedy, we introduce a new concept and model with a well-functioning SupTech system to cope with the May 6 type FinTech crises.</jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.1109/icitsi56531.2022.9970863","title":["SupTech Governance in Regulatory/Supervisory Government Agencies: A Systematic Literature Review"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2022,11,8]]},"container-title":["2022 International Conference on Information Technology Systems and Innovation (ICITSI)"],"type":"proceedings-article"},{"DOI":"10.2139/ssrn.3861890","title":["SupTech: A potential Supervisory Tool of European Banking Supervisors"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2020]]},"container-title":["SSRN Electronic Journal"],"type":"journal-article"},{"DOI":"10.3390/risks10040071","title":["Approaching European Supervisory Risk Assessment with SupTech: A Proposal of an Early Warning System"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2022,3,24]]},"container-title":["Risks"],"type":"journal-article","abstract":"<jats:p>Risk analysis and scenario testing are two of the core activities carried out by economists at central banks. With the increasing adoption of machine learning to enhance decision-support systems, and the amount of collected data spiking, institutions provide countless use-cases for the application of these innovative technologies. Consequently, in recent years, the term sup-tech has entered the financial jargon and is here to stay. In this paper, we address risk assessment from a central bank’s perspective. The uptrending number of involved banks and institutions raises the necessity of a standardised risk methodology. For that reason, we adopted the Risk Assessment Methodology (RAS), the quantitative pillar from the Supervisory Review and Evaluation Process (SREP). Based on real-world supervisory data from the Portuguese banking sector, from March 2014 until August 2021, we successfully model the supervisory risk assessment process, in its quantitative approach by the RAS. Our findings and the resulting model are proposed as an Early Warning System that can support supervisors in their day-to-day tasks, as well as within the SREP process.</jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.1016/j.ijlcj.2026.100830","title":["Addressing challenges in investigating money laundering cases facilitated by underground banking across the Taiwan strait: The role of supervisory technology (SupTech)"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,6]]},"container-title":["International Journal of Law, Crime and Justice"],"type":"journal-article"},{"DOI":"10.1162/99608f92.7a329be7","title":["Digital Transformation of Financial Regulators and the Emergence of Supervisory Technologies (SupTech): A Case Study of the U.K. Financial Conduct Authority"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2023,4,27]]},"container-title":["Harvard Data Science Review"],"type":"journal-article"},{"DOI":"10.4324/9780429325670-24","title":["FinTech, RegTech and SupTech"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2021,4,2]]},"container-title":["Routledge Handbook of Financial Technology and Law"],"type":"book-chapter"},{"DOI":"10.1007/978-981-99-5173-4_6","title":["Overview of SupTech"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2023]]},"container-title":["Contributions to Finance and Accounting","FinTech and SupTech in China"],"type":"book-chapter"},{"DOI":"10.1007/978-981-99-5173-4_8","title":["SupTech Practices by Chinese Regulators"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2023]]},"container-title":["Contributions to Finance and Accounting","FinTech and SupTech in China"],"type":"book-chapter"},{"DOI":"10.1093/law/9780192868763.003.0012","title":["RegTech, SupTech, and Digital Infrastructure"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,2,20]]},"container-title":["Financial Technology and Digital Commercial Law"],"type":"book-chapter","abstract":"<jats:title>Abstract</jats:title>
               <jats:p>This chapter discusses the interplay between RegTech, supervisory technology (SupTech), and digital infrastructure. RegTech encompasses the industry’s use of technology to address regulation and compliance requirements and supervisors’ use of technology to supervise regulatory and compliance requirements. The development of RegTech and SupTech applications responds to growing market needs, addresses the regulatory vacuum, and arms regulators with advanced tools to monitor and oversee compliance. Financial infrastructure is often central to the RegTech/SupTech ecosystem as it is another area where regulators, supervisors, and industry participants use technology to support markets and achieve regulatory and supervisory objectives. The chapter then examines key policy frameworks enabling ecosystem relationships to develop while showcasing vital agile data management and standardization frameworks.</jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.1596/29952","title":["From Spreadsheets to Suptech"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2018,6]]},"type":"book"},{"DOI":"10.1093/he/9780198830559.003.0004","title":["4. Supervisory agencies"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2020,4,23]]},"container-title":["Information Technology Law"],"type":"book-chapter","abstract":"<p>This chapter first describes the rationale for the establishment of supervisory agencies for data protection in EU States. This marks a significant divergence in approach from other countries such as the United States and continues to constitute a barrier to harmonisation in the data protection field. Specific attention is paid to the status and role of the United Kingdom’s Information Commissioner and the investigative and enforcement powers conferred on the Commissioner. The evolving nature of the requirements of registration of data controllers is considered as is the role of the Register of Data Controllers. Attention is given also to the appeal mechanisms established under the Act and to the role of the First Tier Tribunal.</p>"},{"DOI":"10.1007/978-981-99-5173-4_7","title":["Theoretical Issues and Concerns Around SupTech"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2023]]},"container-title":["Contributions to Finance and Accounting","FinTech and SupTech in China"],"type":"book-chapter"},{"DOI":"10.60078/2992-877x-2026-vol4-iss6-pp57-65","title":["REGTECH AND SUPTECH ADOPTION IN THE FINANCIAL REGULATORY ECOSYSTEM:  AN INTEGRATED CONCEPTUAL FRAMEWORK","ВНЕДРЕНИЕ REGTECH И SUPTECH В ЭКОСИСТЕМЕ ФИНАНСОВОГО  РЕГУЛИРОВАНИЯ: ИНТЕГРИРОВАННАЯ КОНЦЕПТУАЛЬНАЯ МОДЕЛЬ","MOLIYAVIY TARTIBGA SOLISH EKOTIZIMIDA REGTECH VA SUPTECH JORIY ETILISHI:  INTEGRATSIYALASHGAN KONSEPTUAL MODEL"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,30]]},"container-title":["Iqtisodiy taraqqiyot va tahlil"],"type":"journal-article","abstract":"<jats:p>The increasing digitalization of financial regulation has accelerated the adoption of Regulatory Technology (RegTech) and Supervisory Technology (SupTech). However, existing studies typically examine these technologies independently, overlooking their interconnected roles within the financial regulatory ecosystem. This study develops an integrated conceptual framework based on the synthesis of existing literature to identify the common drivers and barriers influencing RegTech and SupTech adoption. The framework highlights the interaction between commercial banks and central banks and demonstrates that technology adoption is shaped by enabling and constraining forces operating across the regulatory ecosystem. The framework provides a foundation for future empirical research and policy development</jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.1596/35322","title":["The Next Wave of Suptech Innovation"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2021,3,1]]},"type":"book"},{"DOI":"10.1007/978-981-99-5173-4_9","title":["Policy Suggestions for SupTech Development"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2023]]},"container-title":["Contributions to Finance and Accounting","FinTech and SupTech in China"],"type":"book-chapter"},{"DOI":"10.26163/gief.2025.88.53.036","title":["ПРОБЛЕМЫ ВНЕДРЕНИЯ ЦИФРОВЫХ ТЕХНОЛОГИЙ REGTECH И SUPTECH В ФИНАНСОВУЮ СФЕРУ"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,10,20]]},"container-title":["ЖУРНАЛ ПРАВОВЫХ И ЭКОНОМИЧЕСКИХ ИССЛЕДОВАНИЙ"],"type":"journal-article","abstract":"<jats:p>В статье исследуются проблемы внедрения решений SupTech и RegTech в России. Основное внимание уделяется особенностям интеграции этих технологий в деятельность Центрального банка Российской Федерации. Цель исследования заключается в анализе ключевых характеристик и модельных рисков, связанных с применением SupTech и RegTech, а также в выявлении барьеров и возможностей для их успешного внедрения в российскую финансовую систему.</jats:p>
                  <jats:p>We look at the problems of implementing SupTech and RegTech solutions in Russia. The main focus is on the specific features of integrating these technologies into the activities of the Central Bank of the Russian Federation. The study is aimed at analyzing the key characteristics and model risks connected with the use of SupTech and RegTech as well as identifying barriers and opportunities for their successful implementation in the Russian financial system.</jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.1596/34662","title":["A Roadmap to SupTech Solutions for Low Income (IDA) Countries"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2020,10,7]]},"type":"book"},{"DOI":"10.30574/ijsra.2024.12.2.1614","title":["The necessity of artificial intelligence in fintech for SupTech and RegTech supervisory in banks and financial organizations"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2024,8,30]]},"container-title":["International Journal of Science and Research Archive"],"type":"journal-article","abstract":"<jats:p>In the rapidly evolving financial landscape, the integration of Artificial Intelligence (AI) into Supervisory Technology (SupTech) and Regulatory Technology (RegTech) has become increasingly vital. As banks and financial organizations grapple with the complexities of compliance, risk management, and regulatory oversight, AI offers transformative capabilities that enhance efficiency, accuracy, and resilience. This paper explores the critical need for AI in Fintech for SupTech and RegTech, focusing on its role in supervisory functions within the banking sector. A case study on AI-driven anti-money laundering systems is presented, along with a discussion of authentic laboratory research and survey results. The paper concludes by illustrating the potential impact of AI on financial supervision with graphs and data, underscoring its necessity in modern financial governance.</jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.2139/ssrn.5903962","title":["State of SupTech Report 2025"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025]]},"type":"posted-content","abstract":"<jats:p>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;The &lt;/span&gt;&lt;i&gt;State of SupTech Report 2025&lt;/i&gt;&lt;span&gt; provides a four-year view of how financial authorities are digitalising supervision, drawing on a dataset covering &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;b&gt;312 authorities across 172 countries&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;, including &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;b&gt;148 authorities from 105 countries surveyed in 2025 alone&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;. The report examines suptech adoption, data governance, AI and cloud usage, governance and operating models, and implementation constraints.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Findings show that adoption has accelerated: &lt;span&gt;&lt;b&gt;171 authorities in 107 countries now operate live suptech applications&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/span&gt;, yet the depth and sophistication of deployment remain uneven and strongly correlated with income level and institutional capacity. Uptake is highest in AML/CFT, licensing, and consumer protection, while emerging domains such as AI oversight, climate risk, and digital-assets supervision are gaining attention but remain early-stage. AI progress is cautious, with authorities piloting NLP and predictive analytics while citing persistent constraints related to data quality, privacy, skills, and legacy IT. Across regions, institutions are prioritising foundational data infrastructure, workflow automation, and API-based reporting before scaling advanced analytics and generative AI.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The survey also confirms a strong correlation between &lt;span&gt;&lt;b&gt;strategic clarity&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/span&gt; and &lt;span&gt;&lt;b&gt;scaled implementation&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/span&gt;: authorities with formal data and digital strategies are significantly more likely to deploy multiple applications, while many continue to pursue diagnostics and strategy development in parallel with experimentation on individual applications.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Overall, the report concludes that suptech is entering a phase of deliberate, stepwise modernisation. Closing gaps in data maturity, talent, infrastructure, and governance will be essential for translating technological ambition into sustained supervisory impact.&lt;/p&gt;</jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.4018/978-1-7998-4390-0.ch006","title":["Non-Technological and Technological (SupTech) Innovations in Strengthening the Financial Supervision"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2021]]},"container-title":["Advances in Finance, Accounting, and Economics","Fostering Innovation and Competitiveness With FinTech, RegTech, and SupTech"],"type":"book-chapter","abstract":"<jats:p>The application of non-technological and technological innovations provides support to supervisory institutions in the digitization of reporting and regulatory processes. This chapter deals with international applications of innovations in financial supervision (SupTech). The aim of the chapter is to present the most popular technological and non-technological solutions along with an evaluation of their usefulness in exercising supervision over the financial market. The authors discuss the types of innovations and the reasons for implementing them by supervisory institutions. Furthermore, they describe the most important non-technological supervisory solutions and technologies that supervisors can apply in creating SupTech tools. The study utilizes, among other things, data from reports and elaborations by central banks, supervisory institutions, and consulting companies. The authors' main focus is on the analysis of the solutions utilized by European Union member states.</jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.4337/9781800375956.00024","title":["REGTECH AND SUPTECH: THE FUTURE OF COMPLIANCE"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2021,12,10]]},"container-title":["FinTech"],"type":"book-chapter"}],"items-per-page":25,"query":{"start-index":0,"search-terms":null}}}
